      <div class="lio-lp-review__pick-header">
        <span class="lio-lp-review__pick-rank">#5</span>
        <span class="lio-lp-review__pick-score">7.2<span>/10</span></span>
      </div>
      <h2>Cloudy</h2>
      <p>Cloudy is a newer entrant in the flavored air space, positioned similarly to Lio but with a plastic device body and fewer flavor options. The basic mechanic is the same &mdash; pull air through a scented core &mdash; but build quality is noticeably lower and the flavor experience was inconsistent across our testing panel. Too new to have a meaningful user base or review moat. Worth watching as the brand matures, but not our pick in 2026.</p>
    </div>
  </article>

  <!-- ==================== #6 RIPPLE+ ==================== -->
  <article class="lio-lp-review__pick">
    <div class="lio-lp-review__wrap">
      <div class="lio-lp-review__pick-header">
        <span class="lio-lp-review__pick-rank">#6</span>
        <span class="lio-lp-review__pick-score">6.8<span>/10</span></span>
      </div>
      <h2>Ripple+</h2>
      <p>Here&rsquo;s where we get controversial: Ripple+ is marketed alongside flavored air devices, but it&rsquo;s actually an electronic vape that heats a plant-based liquid into visible vapor. It has a battery. It has a heating coil. It produces exhalable vapor &mdash; they even market the &ldquo;visible puff&rdquo; as a feature.</p>
      <p>If you want something that looks and feels exactly like a vape, Ripple+ delivers. But if your goal is to actually break the vaping habit &mdash; rather than switch to a different vape with no nicotine &mdash; Ripple+ may reinforce the exact behavior you&rsquo;re trying to end. You also can&rsquo;t use it on planes, in many offices, or anywhere vapes are restricted. Pods run ~$15 each and last about 500 puffs, so the ongoing cost adds up fast.</p>
      <p><strong>Verdict:</strong> If you&rsquo;re committed to ending the vape ritual, Ripple+ is the wrong tool. If you just want a nicotine-free vape, it&rsquo;s a solid one.</p>
    </div>
  </article>

  <!-- ==================== #7 LUVV ==================== -->
  <article class="lio-lp-review__pick">
    <div class="lio-lp-review__wrap">
      <div class="lio-lp-review__pick-header">
        <span class="lio-lp-review__pick-rank">#7</span>
        <span class="lio-lp-review__pick-score">6.3<span>/10</span></span>
      </div>
      <h2>LUVV</h2>
      <p>LUVV sells electronic disposable &ldquo;vitamin vapes&rdquo; &mdash; battery-powered devices that heat a water-soluble vitamin formula to ~240°F to create breathable vapor. Like Ripple+, these are technically e-cigarettes (minus the nicotine), not flavored air devices in the purist sense. The vitamin angle (B12, melatonin, chamomile, collagen) is an interesting wellness positioning, but the underlying mechanism is a heated battery-powered vape.</p>
      <p>For users whose goal is vitamin inhalation as a supplement, LUVV is a legitimate niche product. For users whose goal is to break a vaping or smoking ritual, you&rsquo;re buying a different vape. Same concerns as Ripple+: can&rsquo;t use on planes, heats and inhales aerosol, battery dies.</p>
    </div>
  </article>

